{
  "content": {
    "arguments": "$ARGUMENTS",
    "constraints": [
      {
        "id": "1bf67419-3376-4891-9513-cffffa8578a9",
        "text": "Use the Bash tool. The Pebbles CLI at \"{PEBBLES_CLI}\" is the ONLY sanctioned way to change spec files — never edit .pebble files directly."
      },
      {
        "id": "8f4c9090-fbb7-4e9a-9b11-c7b61f6a5d8b",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Verification — standard (rules/system/verification-standard)"
      },
      {
        "id": "794ec81c-c6dc-43cd-b5a7-3cbcc8d9bf94",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Spec mutation contract (rules/system/spec-mutation-contract)"
      },
      {
        "id": "42b05336-6cfd-46bd-8e3d-ee2a9f7198d9",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Status discipline (rules/system/status-discipline)"
      },
      {
        "id": "5b1fa77c-8498-4339-bc37-21fa95bb4443",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Findings are the append-only evidence log (rules/system/findings-as-evidence)"
      },
      {
        "id": "64377f3c-56c5-440d-8d77-2fc52ef68a73",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Graph-first navigation (rules/system/graph-first-navigation)"
      },
      {
        "id": "cb2d4f59-10ad-4a84-9bc3-72c35654199d",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Command discipline (rules/system/command-discipline)"
      }
    ],
    "examples": [],
    "kind": "skill",
    "onFailure": [
      {
        "id": "f585be13-4cd3-4ffd-b15e-f589305a1658",
        "text": "Read the CLI error — it names the fix; correct the call and retry once"
      },
      {
        "id": "4bb5664d-9949-4b70-bfe6-79a2b7b5cf76",
        "text": "NEVER fall back to editing .pebble files directly, whatever failed"
      },
      {
        "id": "ebcf1572-f144-4cb2-8281-327b8ca88b93",
        "text": "If still blocked, report the exact command and error to the user and stop"
      }
    ],
    "output": "A per-unit finding (verified-working with evidence, or a defect with severity) AND a truthful per-unit status for every unit checked; the pebble rolled up to its worst unit; a coverage statement.",
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "id": "705d8478-d860-4045-8f63-732fa34d9333",
        "text": "The pebble is read and its UNITS enumerated first (spec-first) — nodes/items/fields/endpoints + its refs, rules and architecture"
      },
      {
        "id": "4fd0f12a-221e-4c81-a881-cd5e09de4dbf",
        "text": "Each unit's implementation is locatable: codeLinks first, else map-symbol/map-search (models: migrations/DDL/SQL even with no ORM)"
      }
    ],
    "purpose": "Verify a spec against the actual code (records findings)",
    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "f83739e9-0cf0-4924-9ea8-e7a269710435",
        "text": "Verify the code complies with this spec — start from the PEBBLE (the spec), not the code: $ARGUMENTS"
      },
      {
        "id": "a1ff7aa9-a92f-4c62-aa9a-bc6a1fd0f675",
        "text": "Covers ANYTHING that needs verifying, whatever its origin — a spec the user hand-wrote and implemented himself, or any other; hand-coded or generated; workflows, models, structures, apis, requirements, tests. The question is always compliance: does the code deliver what the spec requires? If the SPEC itself looks wrong, file it as an assumption/spec-violation finding for the user — never silently pass, never fix."
      },
      {
        "id": "51816a81-2cec-4351-bad1-71419bd4bcbb",
        "text": "READ-ONLY: this skill NEVER edits application code or spec content — it only reads, judges, sets statuses and files findings with evidence. If a fix is needed, record it as a finding and hand it to /pebbles-fix; do NOT verify-and-fix in one pass."
      },
      {
        "id": "8df53cc6-57e8-4c0e-acf1-7231e710bd8f",
        "text": "STEP 0 — LOAD THE COMPLETE CONTEXT, then enumerate (echo before judging): the pre-loaded chat context may be CAPPED, so fetch the full set yourself — `get` the FULL pebble, `refs` it BOTH ways, and resolve EVERY ref and codeLink (never rely on a partial digest). List EVERY unit it holds and load its connections: the requirements it serves, the governing rules/ (enforcement levels matter) and architecture/ boundaries. That set is the contract. A verification on partial context, or with no enumerated unit list, is a vibe check."
      },
      {
        "id": "ed793214-affd-47a4-9a46-07e45fa20974",
        "text": "STEP 1 — LOCATE per unit: implementation via codeLinks, else map-symbol/map-search. For a MODEL/persisted data, find the storage truth even with NO ORM — the migrations (walk the full history for that table), DDL, or raw SQL. No code located for a unit → status missing + a finding; never assume a pass."
      },
      {
        "id": "c379a38d-7b6e-4d2e-b478-5ba1391c5de1",
        "text": "STEP 2 — VALIDATE EVERY UNIT, ONE BY ONE — NO exceptions, NO sampling, NO caps, NO \"representative subset\" (don't advance until the current one is judged): does the implementation satisfy EVERYTHING attached to this unit — its own description AND the requirements it serves AND every governing rule (strict = MUST — a violation is a finding even if the code \"works\") AND the architecture boundaries AND the apis/models it must honor? Chase every reference to its definition; an unread reference is an unfinished validation. Verify BEHAVIOR, not existence. JUDGE BY THE UNIT'S CLAIM (its label + description + refs), NOT by any drawing convention — you know how a workflow is normally drawn but the spec need not follow it; node/unit usage is FREE, so validate whatever intent the unit states in whatever shape the author chose, and handle the most unusual use case on its own terms. A whole behavior modeled as ONE process node that DESCRIBES everything — no branch/condition/try-catch/outcome nodes at all — is valid: read its claim and validate ALL of it against the code, as thoroughly as a decomposed graph (alignment of spec↔code is the test; a missing convention is never a finding). EVERY node counts, of EVERY kind and role — none privileged, none scenery. WORKFLOW: walk EVERY node in FLOW ORDER — start, every step, every condition arm, every parallel branch, every subprocess (recurse), AND every terminal/outcome node (success/error/exception/external alike). TRY/CATCH (the dedicated `try`/`catch` nodes): given a `try` (BODY + SPINE-to-catch outputs) and its paired `catch` (an \"Exceptions\" line + an unlabeled continuation), verify against the code that a real guarded try/catch (in whatever form the language uses — you identify it, not by keyword) opens where the try says and closes at the catch; the BODY nodes match the try block's statements in order and the last returns to the catch; each INNER GUARD is verified individually (its own condition + any throw its own `error` \"… error thrown\" — both arms vs the code, never waved off as \"inside the try\"); the \"Exceptions\" line matches the code's catch/rescue — follow it and verify the failure handling NODE-FOR-NODE (every rollback/log/notify/mark-failed/rethrow node maps to a real catch action and every real catch action has a node — code-but-no-node is `missing`, node-but-no-code is `drift`); CAUGHT vs UNCAUGHT is correct — a catch that handles locally ends at an end with NO global-exception (an invented one is `drift`), a rethrow/escape hands off via an `exception` node into a `global-exception` flow (a missing one for a real escape is `missing`; verify that flow too); the CONTINUATION line (no label) matches the code after the try/catch, reached when no exception is thrown; every path terminates. If the control-flow reading is unclear (where the try closes, which line is the catch vs the continuation, whether a rollback truly runs), CONSULT the workflow expert before labeling. MODEL: field by field vs the storage truth. CHECKLIST: each item is a REQUIREMENT the functionality must meet to pass verification — met with proof → verified; NOT met → CHANGE its status to what is true (missing when nothing implements it, broken/drift when the implementation contradicts it) + a finding; an unmet requirement never stays looking passed. The count you validated MUST equal the total node/unit count; if they differ you are not done."
      },
      {
        "id": "4f6a0b33-b93a-48ea-99fe-38abffde188b",
        "text": "STEP 3 — GET THE VERDICT RIGHT, then RECORD PER UNIT (always, pass or fail): a confident wrong status is worse than none — when unsure about a domain, CONSULT the relevant expert and let their judgment settle it before labeling (control-flow → workflow expert · schema/migrations/FKs → data-modeler · architecture boundaries → architect · auth/exposure/injection → sentinel · external contracts → integration expert). An uncertain verdict is not a verdict. Then: \"{PEBBLES_CLI}\" add-finding <path> --title \"...\" --unit <id> --category verified-working|bug|spec-violation|security|performance|regression --severity low|medium|high --body \"## Observation"
      },
      {
        "id": "67db765c-1888-4b7b-8541-edc253d2c4df",
        "text": "..."
      },
      {
        "id": "ddaa07d2-f04f-4507-9f51-e972f1b56bfd",
        "text": "## Evidence"
      },
      {
        "id": "b673faa4-9975-46d0-9060-2118a4fba305",
        "text": "- file:line — proof\". WRITE THE OBSERVATION IN TWO TIERS: (1) PLAIN LANGUAGE first — what you checked and, if wrong, WHAT is wrong + WHY it matters, readable by product/QA; (2) TECHNICAL last, a separate paragraph — file:line, symbols, the exact mismatch. The ISSUE goes in the FINDING, NEVER in the node/pebble description (you never edit a description to say something is wrong). And \"{PEBBLES_CLI}\" set-status <path> <status> --unit <id> from the worst finding on it (clean→verified · claims-but-absent→missing · wrong/partial→drift · high-severity defect→broken). A node you genuinely cannot validate is set missing/unverifiable WITH a finding — accounted for, NEVER silently omitted. Supersede a prior finding on the same unit, never edit it."
      },
      {
        "id": "d2f37414-0849-4c86-b40f-236783e6a7ae",
        "text": "STEP 3b — REVERSE GAP (code the spec does NOT cover): after the units, scan the SAME code span for real behavior with NO corresponding node/item — an extra branch, an error path, a side effect, a whole feature in code but absent from the spec. Record each as a PEBBLE-level finding (\"{PEBBLES_CLI}\" add-finding <path> --category spec-violation|tech-debt|other --body \"## Observation"
      },
      {
        "id": "2c26ed8a-f99f-4c07-9c6d-c81f0079c42d",
        "text": "code does X …"
      },
      {
        "id": "347be298-e8be-4369-9890-9c225e290acb",
        "text": "## Evidence"
      },
      {
        "id": "b4cbb1ae-3896-4194-8378-5c8974000a2b",
        "text": "- file:line\") naming what the code does + where and that the spec lacks it. Do NOT add the node yourself — verify is READ-ONLY on spec structure (its only writes are per-unit statuses + findings); the finding lets the USER update the workflow. A spec is trustworthy only if it covers what actually runs. STATUS: a node the spec claims but code lacks = that node `missing` (spec ahead of code); code that exists but the spec omits has no node to status → record the finding AND roll the PEBBLE to at least `drift` (code ahead of spec — reconcile) even if every drawn node verified. No new status is needed — `missing` and `drift` are the two directions."
      },
      {
        "id": "51c9d838-cade-4f3e-9534-eac168b0cd0a",
        "text": "STEP 4 — CONFIRM & ROLL UP: `get` the pebble back and confirm EVERY unit now carries a verify status — none left planned/untouched (verified-count == total-node-count). Only then set the pebble's status to the WORST unit status (most severe wins) and file one summary finding. The explorer colors the file from that automatically."
      },
      {
        "id": "acdc4494-f6e5-446a-a13c-cd9fe3dcef9a",
        "text": "Report: a coverage LEDGER — list every node with its verdict and prove verified-count == total-node-count (no node missing from the list); then the per-unit evidence and the pebble's rolled-up status."
      }
    ],
    "verify": [
      {
        "id": "6477fcd1-928b-4ab1-84e4-e67558c15aa2",
        "text": "EVERY node/unit verified — the verified count EQUALS the total; nothing sampled, capped, or silently skipped (a genuinely unverifiable unit is set missing/unverifiable WITH a finding, never omitted)"
      },
      {
        "id": "119facb5-0d5f-47d0-b2d2-069b4f874717",
        "text": "Every node verified individually regardless of kind or role — steps, conditions, parallels, subprocesses, and every terminal/outcome (success paths and failure paths alike); none privileged, none skipped, down to the smallest"
      },
      {
        "id": "cd8776fd-d75b-4117-ab02-0b32e61eb6b5",
        "text": "Each unit proven against real BEHAVIOR (not symbol presence); workflows walked in flow order incl. subprocesses; models checked field by field vs migrations/DDL"
      },
      {
        "id": "1aa5fa3b-ba27-4ae8-8452-7da41dc6925c",
        "text": "Each unit has BOTH a per-unit finding AND a truthful per-unit status; the pebble rolled up to its WORST unit; a summary finding filed"
      }
    ],
    "whenToUse": "The correctness gate — the REVERSE of extract: start from the pebble, check EVERY unit (nodes/items/fields) against its specs (requirements, rules, architecture), and prove each is implemented correctly."
  },
